# Line endings are LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. This project is bash + bats + markdown, and it is
# developed on Windows where `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git default. With CRLF in
# the working tree:
#
#   * WSL and any Linux shell REFUSE to run the scripts and tests at all —
#     `$'\r': command not found`, `syntax error near unexpected token $'{\r'`.
#     That matters because the test suite needs POSIX file modes, and Git Bash on
#     NTFS cannot provide them (its MSYS mounts are `noacl`, so chmod is a silent
#     no-op: `chmod 700` reports 755). WSL is the only local environment that can
#     run the mode-dependent suites, and CRLF was what locked it out.
#   * shellcheck reports SC1017 ("literal carriage return") on EVERY file,
#     including untouched ones, which makes the step-13 analyzer output
#     indistinguishable from real findings.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect and leave binary content alone, so this is
# safe for any future non-text file.
* text=auto eol=lf
